        <h2>Summary / Description</h2>
        <div class="description">
          <p>OBJECTIVES:<p>
Although various effects of microgravity at the whole plant, organ, tissue, and cellular
levels have been observed, little is known about the molecular mechanisms involved. This
experiment studied the effects of microgravity on the expression of auxin-regulated GH3
and SAUR genes in transgenic tomato, tobacco and Arabidopsis seedlings that express the
auxin-inducible GH3 gene promoter-GUS and SAUR gene promoter-GUS fusion genes.<br>
<br>
This was the second flight of this experiment and there were four objectives. The first
objective was to determine how the expression of the auxin- and gravistimulation-inducible
genes (e.g. GH3 and SAUR genes) is altered under the microgravity environment. The second
objective was to identify the effects of microgravity on auxin transport/distribution
and/or tissue&#39s sensitivity to auxin in higher plants. Identification of the genes whose
expression is significantly altered in microgravity grown plants (in addition to the auxin
inducible genes) was the third objective. The fourth was to determine the functions of the
genes whose expression is altered in plants grown in microgravity.<br>
<br>
This experiment provided information about how expression of the auxin- and
gravistimulation-inducible genes is altered at microgravity as compared to the ground
controls. It also provided some insights into the effects of microgravity on auxin
transport/distribution and/or tissue&#39s sensitivity to auxin in higher plants. Molecular
cloning and characterization of gravity-regulated genes is a crucial step to elucidate the
mechanisms of gravity effects on growth, development, and metabolic processes in higher
plants.<p>APPROACH:<p>
The BRIC-10 experiment flew on the STS-85 mission which lasted approximately 12 days. It
was launched aboard Discovery on August 19, 1997. The experiment was placed in two BRIC-60
canisters, each containing eleven 60 mm petri plates. Transgenic tomato, tobacco and
Arabidopsis seedlings were grown on the petri plates, which contained five layers of
filter paper and one layer of nylon membrane to support seedlings and nutrient media. At 5
dayts into the flight (MET 5/02:10) one of the BRIC-60 canisters was frozen in the LSLE
GN2 Freezer. Ground controls were conducted on a 24-hour delay in the Orbiter
Environmental Simulator at environmental conditions (temperature, % relative humidity, and
CO2) similar to those experienced by the flight samples.<br>
<br>
Upon recovery, the flight samples frozen in space were treated in the following fashion:
forty representative seedlings were fixed in a solution containing 5% formaldehyde for
microscopic analysis; ten seedlings were photographed; and the remainder were returned to
the investigator&#39s lab on dry ice for RNA isolation and screening for the genes of
interest.<br>
<br>
The flight samples which were not frozen in space were treated in the following fashion:
three to four representative seedlings from each petri dish were photographed with a
camera coupled to a microscope; forty seedlings from each petri dish were incubated in
x-gluc solution for histochemical staining of the GUS activity at 37C for 12 to 24 hours,
and after staining, the seedlings were photographed. All seedlings stained with x-gluc
solution were stored in water with sodium azide for further analysis at the investigator&#39s
lab. Thirty seedlings from each petri dish were fixed in 5% formaldehyde solution for
microscopic analysis. Between sixty and one hundred and fifty seedlings (50 seedlings per
assay, 3 replicates) from each petri dish were frozen at -70C for quantitative assay of
GUS activity.<p>RESULTS:<p>
The results of this study indicate that the expression of both the GH3 promoter-GUS and
SAUR promoter-GUS fusion genes was significantly enhanced (2 to 3-fold increases) in
space-grown seedlings when compared with the ground control seedlings. The activation of
the GH3 promoter-GUS fusion gene in tobacco plants was dependent on the developmental
stage, initially with an activation in cotyledons, followed by activation in elongating
regions of hypocotyls and roots of the space-grown seedlings. As seedlings developed, the
expression of the gene was reduced in cotyledons but further enhanced in root elongating
regions. The activation of the GH3 promoter-GUS gene was relatively higher in vascular
tissues and much lower in other tissue types. The expression of the SAUR promoter-GUS gene
in the elongating region of hypocotyls in the space-grown seedlings was also significantly
higher than in the ground controls. The activation of the GH3 promoter-GUS and SAUR
promoter-GUS genes in space-grown plants strongly suggests that micro-gravity affects
auxin transport/distribution or tissue&#39s sensitivity to auxin.<br>
<br>
The space-grown seedlings from the BRIC-9 experiment had shorter roots and more and longer
root hairs. Increased auxin concentration or tissue&#39s sensitivity to auxin in elongating
regions of roots as indicated by activation of the GH3 promoter-GUS gene, suggests that
auxin is involved in the micro-gravity mediated reduction in root growth and increases in
root hair elongation.<br>
<br>
We have isolated mRNA from both the space-grown and ground control seedlings of
Arabidopsis. A cDNA library has been constructed and we are currently in the process of
screening genes whose expression is altered in space-grown seedlings. Once the genes of
interest are recovered, the investigators will perform sequence analysis, Northern blot
analysis and other analysis.<br>
</p>
